<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capetian House of Anjou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Angevins of Naples, Angevin dynasty of Naples</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Capetian House of Anjou was a branch of the French royal family founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, brother of Louis IX. Charles conquered the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1266 but lost the island in the Sicilian Vespers of 1282, after which his descendants ruled the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> until 1435. Another line, beginning with Charles Robert in 1308, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis the Great</a>, also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou.jpg" alt="Capetian House of Anjou" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlodangio · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Jean_dAnjou.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in the Drassanes of Barcelona and commanded by Roger of Lauria won a series of victories against the <b>Angevins</b> after Peter III took Sicily in 1282: off Malta in 1283, in the Bay of Naples in 1284, where Charles of Salerno was captured, and at Les Formigues in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Late medieval Hungary, rebuilt by the <b>Angevins</b> and enriched by Europe's richest gold mines, was a leading central European power and the chief barrier against Ottoman expansion; under Matthias Corvinus it became an early northern home of the Renaissance, before magnate rule and defeat at Mohács in 1526…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom inherited from the Normans and Frederick II a centralised royal administration, with justiciars over the provinces, a royal treasury and a high court, but the <b>Angevins</b> granted extensive lands and jurisdiction to French and local barons, and royal authority depended heavily on these lords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sicilian Vespers, which broke out in Palermo in March 1282, began twenty years of war between the <b>Angevins of Naples</b> and the Aragonese of Sicily, in which Roger of Lauria, the Italian-born admiral of the Aragonese fleet, won repeated naval victories; the Peace of Caltabellotta of 1302…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The admiral Roger of Lauria won a series of victories against the <b>Angevins</b> and the French, and when Philip III of France invaded Catalonia in the Aragonese Crusade of 1285, the French army was forced to retreat after its fleet was destroyed at the Battle of Les Formigues.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By the thirteenth century the city was splitting into factions, the Guelphs, who looked to the papacy and the <b>Angevins</b>, and the Ghibellines, who supported the emperors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
